PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT
PoC: Python Desktop 3D OSM Map Renderer with Simulated Vehicle Route
Area: Tokyo, Shinagawa / Takanawa / Konan / Osaki / Gotanda vicinity
Document version: 1.1
Date: 2026-07-02
Author: ChatGPT for Lenik Terenin
Revised: 2026-07-02 — resolved window library, follow-camera UX, text overlay scope, starting state, mouse controls, speed hotkeys, missing config fields, and minor structural gaps (see revision notes in each section)

================================================================================
1. PROJECT SUMMARY
================================================================================

Build a desktop Proof of Concept application in Python that:

1. Downloads or loads a small subset of OpenStreetMap data around Tokyo's Shinagawa area.
2. Preprocesses the data into lightweight local render assets.
3. Renders a simplified 3D map with roads and buildings using OpenGL from Python.
4. Animates a marker, representing a vehicle, along a predefined 4-5 km closed-loop route.
5. Demonstrates a stable interactive camera and smooth playback at a target of 30-60 FPS on a normal laptop.

This is not intended to be a full navigation app, routing engine, or production-quality map renderer. The goal is to validate whether a Python + OpenGL stack can support a visually clear local 3D map and route-following simulation using real OSM-derived geometry.

Recommended PoC stack:

- Python 3.11+
- OSMnx for OSM street/building retrieval and graph processing
- GeoPandas / Shapely for geospatial preprocessing
- pyproj for coordinate projection
- NumPy for geometry buffers
- ModernGL for OpenGL rendering
- moderngl-window for window/context/input (chosen; see FR-CAM-001)
- Optional: networkx for route graph operations if not using OSMnx helper methods directly

Key reference links:

- OSMnx documentation: https://osmnx.readthedocs.io/
- ModernGL documentation: https://moderngl.readthedocs.io/
- ModernGL GitHub: https://github.com/moderngl/moderngl
- Overpass bounding-box documentation: https://dev.overpass-api.de/overpass-doc/en/full_data/bbox.html
- Overpass API / Overpass QL: https://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Overpass_API/Overpass_QL
- pyproj documentation: https://pyproj4.github.io/pyproj/
- OpenStreetMap elements: https://wiki.openstreetmap.org/wiki/Elements

================================================================================
2. GOALS AND NON-GOALS
================================================================================

2.1 Goals
---------

G1. Load a real OSM-derived street network for a small Shinagawa-area bounding box.

G2. Load building footprints in the same area, extrude them into simple 3D blocks, and render them together with roads.

G3. Preprocess raw geospatial data into local coordinate geometry suitable for real-time rendering.

G4. Define or auto-generate a 4-5 km closed-loop vehicle route on drivable streets.

G5. Animate a marker along this route at a configurable simulated speed, e.g. 30 km/h, 40 km/h, 50 km/h.

G6. Display the route itself as a highlighted path on top of the road layer.

G7. Allow basic camera control:

- pan / orbit / rotate around the map;
- zoom in/out;
- optional follow-vehicle camera;
- optional top-down and oblique 3D view modes.

G8. Achieve smooth rendering:

- minimum acceptable: 30 FPS on a normal laptop;
- target: 60 FPS with the default dataset;
- avoid visible stutters during normal playback after preprocessing has completed.

G9. Persist preprocessed local assets so that subsequent app starts do not need to re-download or reprocess OSM data.

G10. Keep the code structured so C++/Rust acceleration can later be introduced for geometry preprocessing without rewriting the whole application.

2.2 Non-goals
-------------

NG1. No live turn-by-turn navigation.

NG2. No live GPS support in PoC scope.

NG3. No large-scale map streaming. Only one local area is required.

NG4. No production-grade cartographic styling.

NG5. No advanced label rendering in the first PoC version.

NG6. No terrain elevation in the first PoC version.

NG7. No realistic traffic simulation.

NG8. No realistic vehicle physics; the marker simply moves along a polyline path.

NG9. No map matching against noisy GPS data in this PoC.

NG10. No mobile support.

NG11. No packaging for Windows/macOS/Linux installers unless added as a stretch goal.

================================================================================
3. TARGET USER / USE CASE
================================================================================

Primary user:

- Developer / technical evaluator who wants to test Python's feasibility for a desktop 3D OSM renderer.

Primary scenario:

1. Developer runs a preprocessing script.
2. Script downloads or loads OSM data for the Shinagawa area.
3. Script creates local render assets: roads, buildings, route, metadata.
4. Developer starts the renderer.
5. App opens a 3D scene showing roads and building blocks.
6. A vehicle marker drives around a closed loop for 4-5 km.
7. Developer can move the camera, pause/resume the marker, and inspect FPS.

================================================================================
4. AREA OF INTEREST
================================================================================

4.1 Recommended PoC Area
------------------------

Use a small but dense urban area around Shinagawa Station. This gives enough buildings, roads, overpasses, and visual complexity without becoming too large.

Recommended approximate center:

- Shinagawa Station area
- Latitude: 35.6285
- Longitude: 139.7388

Recommended initial bounding box:

- North latitude: 35.6420
- South latitude: 35.6150
- East longitude: 139.7600
- West longitude: 139.7150

Approximate coverage:

- north-south: about 3.0 km
- east-west: about 4.0 km

This covers a useful area around Shinagawa, Takanawa, Konan, Osaki, and possibly Gotanda depending on exact bounds.

4.2 Alternative Smaller Debug Area
----------------------------------

For performance/debug runs, use a smaller bounding box:

- North latitude: 35.6350
- South latitude: 35.6220
- East longitude: 139.7500
- West longitude: 139.7270

This smaller area should load faster and is useful while developing the renderer.

4.3 Coordinate System
---------------------

Raw OSM data uses WGS84 latitude/longitude.

For rendering, convert all geometry into a local metric coordinate system.

Recommended approach:

1. Use pyproj to project WGS84 coordinates into a metric projection.
2. For Tokyo, use UTM zone 54N or a suitable Japanese plane rectangular coordinate system.
3. Subtract a local origin near the bounding box center so OpenGL coordinates stay small.

Recommended PoC projection:

- EPSG:4326 input, WGS84 lat/lon
- EPSG:32654 output, WGS84 / UTM zone 54N

Local rendering coordinate convention:

- X: east-west meters, positive east
- Y: up, vertical meters
- Z: north-south meters, positive north

After projection:

- world_x = projected_easting - origin_easting
- world_z = projected_northing - origin_northing
- world_y = elevation/height

================================================================================
5. DATA SOURCES
================================================================================

5.1 Preferred Data Source for PoC
---------------------------------

Use OSMnx to retrieve data from OpenStreetMap / Overpass.

Required OSM data categories:

1. Drivable road network
2. Building footprints
3. Optional water/green/rail layers if easy, but not required for MVP

OSMnx can download and model street networks and other geospatial features from OpenStreetMap. It is suitable for this PoC because it can retrieve driving networks and building footprints with relatively little code.

5.2 Alternative Data Loading Method
-----------------------------------

For more reproducible tests, use a downloaded OSM PBF extract or cached GeoPackage/GraphML instead of querying Overpass every time.

Possible sources:

- OSMnx cache
- manually downloaded Geofabrik Japan/Kanto extract
- local clipped .osm.pbf made using osmium
- saved GraphML for road graph
- saved GeoPackage/Parquet/GeoJSON for buildings

PoC should support at least one cache path and avoid repeated Overpass requests during normal development.

5.3 Data Licensing Note
-----------------------

OpenStreetMap data is licensed under the Open Database License. Any public distribution using OSM-derived data should include proper attribution. For this private PoC, still display attribution in the UI or README:

"Map data © OpenStreetMap contributors"

