6.3 Runtime Data Flow
---------------------

Preprocessing flow:

1. Read config bounding box.
2. Download or load road graph.
3. Download or load building footprints.
4. Project all coordinates to local metric coordinates.
5. Convert road centerlines into renderable line/strip geometry.
6. Convert building footprints into extruded 3D mesh geometry.
7. Generate or validate route polyline.
8. Save processed arrays to local disk.

Runtime flow:

1. Load processed asset metadata.
2. Load road, building, route, and vehicle meshes into NumPy arrays.
3. Upload arrays to GPU buffers.
4. Start render loop.
5. Update marker position along route based on elapsed simulation time.
6. Draw roads, buildings, route, marker, and debug overlay each frame.

================================================================================
7. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

7.1 Data Acquisition Requirements
---------------------------------

FR-DATA-001: The preprocessing script shall retrieve a drivable street network for the configured Shinagawa bounding box.

FR-DATA-002: The preprocessing script shall retrieve building footprints for the same bounding box.

FR-DATA-003: The system shall cache downloaded data locally to avoid repeated requests to the OSM/Overpass servers.

FR-DATA-004: The system shall allow offline reruns using cached raw data.

FR-DATA-005: The script shall log the bounding box, number of graph nodes, number of edges, number of building footprints, and output file paths.

FR-DATA-006: The script shall handle missing building height tags gracefully.

FR-DATA-007: The script shall ignore invalid or impossible geometries rather than crashing.

FR-DATA-008: The script shall save a metadata JSON file containing:

- data timestamp;
- bounding box;
- projection EPSG;
- local origin lat/lon;
- local origin projected coordinates;
- counts of roads/buildings/vertices/triangles;
- route length in meters;
- preprocessing version.

7.2 Road Preprocessing Requirements
-----------------------------------

FR-ROAD-001: The road layer shall include drivable roads suitable for vehicle simulation.

FR-ROAD-002: Each road segment shall be converted from lat/lon to local X/Z meter coordinates.

FR-ROAD-003: Road visual geometry shall be generated as strips, not raw GL_LINES, if the desired visual width should remain stable and clear.

FR-ROAD-004: Road width shall be determined from OSM tags where possible, with fallbacks by highway type.

Suggested road width fallback table:

- motorway / trunk: 12.0 m
- primary: 10.0 m
- secondary: 8.0 m
- tertiary: 7.0 m
- residential: 5.5 m
- service: 4.0 m
- unclassified: 5.0 m
- default: 5.0 m

FR-ROAD-005: Road mesh generation shall support polylines with more than two points.

FR-ROAD-006: For the MVP, intersections may have visual overlap artifacts. Perfect road joins are not required.

FR-ROAD-007: Road vertices shall include at minimum:

- position: x, y, z
- optional color/style id
- optional road type id

FR-ROAD-008: Road mesh shall be saved in a render-ready format such as .npz:

- road_vertices: float32 array, shape [N, 3] or [N, 6]
- road_indices: uint32 array, shape [M]
- road_metadata: JSON-compatible list or sidecar file

7.3 Building Preprocessing Requirements
---------------------------------------

FR-BLDG-001: Building footprints shall be loaded from OSM building tags.

FR-BLDG-002: Building footprints shall be projected into local metric coordinates.

FR-BLDG-003: Invalid polygons shall be fixed if possible using Shapely tools or skipped with a warning.

FR-BLDG-004: Each building shall be extruded into a simple 3D mesh.

FR-BLDG-005: Building height shall be determined using this priority:

1. OSM height tag, if available and parseable.
2. OSM building:levels tag multiplied by default floor height.
3. Default random or deterministic fallback height.

Suggested height rules:

- floor height: 3.0 m
- default building height: 9.0 m
- maximum fallback random height: 18.0 m
- clamp explicit heights to 3.0-250.0 m for visual sanity

FR-BLDG-006: Building extrusion shall produce wall triangles and roof triangles.

FR-BLDG-007: For MVP, complex roofs are not required. Flat roofs are enough.

FR-BLDG-008: For MVP, holes in building polygons may be ignored, but this limitation must be documented.

FR-BLDG-009: For better quality, polygon triangulation should support holes in later versions.

FR-BLDG-010: Building vertices shall include at minimum:

- position: x, y, z
- normal: nx, ny, nz, if simple lighting is implemented
- optional building id/style id

FR-BLDG-011: Building mesh shall be saved in a render-ready format such as .npz:

- building_vertices: float32 array
- building_indices: uint32 array
- building_metadata: JSON-compatible list or sidecar file

7.4 Route Requirements
----------------------

FR-ROUTE-001: The PoC shall include one predefined 4-5 km route.

FR-ROUTE-002: The route should be a closed loop or near-closed loop.

FR-ROUTE-003: The route shall follow the OSM drivable road graph.

FR-ROUTE-004: The route shall be stored as an ordered list of projected local points and optionally original OSM graph node IDs.

FR-ROUTE-005: The route file shall include cumulative distance along the route for fast marker interpolation.

FR-ROUTE-006: The route shall be highlighted visually in the renderer.

FR-ROUTE-007: The marker shall move continuously along the route and wrap back to the beginning when the end is reached.

FR-ROUTE-008: The initial route speed shall be configurable.

Suggested route simulation speeds:

- default: 40 km/h
- debug slow: 10 km/h
- fast demo: 80 km/h

FR-ROUTE-009: The user shall be able to pause/resume route animation.

FR-ROUTE-010: The user shall be able to reset the marker to the start of the route.

7.5 Suggested Route Around Shinagawa
------------------------------------

The exact route should be generated against the downloaded OSM graph, so the implementation should not rely only on manually listed coordinates. However, the following approximate loop can be used as target waypoints:

A. Shinagawa Station / Takanawa Gateway vicinity
   approx lat/lon: 35.6285, 139.7388

B. Takanawa / Sengakuji side
   approx lat/lon: 35.6370, 139.7380

C. Konan / Shinagawa Intercity side
   approx lat/lon: 35.6265, 139.7475

D. Osaki / Gotanda direction
   approx lat/lon: 35.6200, 139.7285

E. Return to Shinagawa Station vicinity
   approx lat/lon: 35.6285, 139.7388

Implementation should:

1. Snap these waypoints to nearest graph nodes.
2. Compute shortest paths between consecutive waypoints.
3. Concatenate those paths.
4. Measure total route length.
5. If route is shorter than 4 km, expand the waypoint set.
6. If route is longer than 5.5 km, use a smaller loop or adjust waypoints.
7. Save the final route.

Alternative algorithm:

1. Pick a starting graph node near Shinagawa Station.
2. Search for candidate nodes 1.0-1.5 km away in several directions.
3. Build a loop using shortest paths between candidates.
4. Score candidates by total length close to 4.5 km and low repeated-edge count.
5. Save the best route.

7.6 Rendering Requirements
--------------------------

FR-RENDER-001: The renderer shall use OpenGL through Python.

FR-RENDER-002: Preferred rendering library is ModernGL.

FR-RENDER-003: The app shall open a desktop window of at least 1280 x 720.

FR-RENDER-004: The app shall render:

- background plane;
- roads;
- buildings;
- highlighted route;
- moving vehicle marker;
- basic FPS/status overlay.

FR-RENDER-005: Roads and buildings shall be uploaded to GPU buffers once at startup, not rebuilt every frame.

FR-RENDER-006: The per-frame loop shall avoid per-object Python draw calls for individual buildings or road segments.

FR-RENDER-007: The renderer shall batch roads into one or a small number of draw calls.

FR-RENDER-008: The renderer shall batch buildings into one or a small number of draw calls.

FR-RENDER-009: The marker may be updated each frame using a small dynamic transform matrix or small dynamic vertex buffer.

FR-RENDER-010: The route highlight shall be rendered as a separate strip or line mesh.

FR-RENDER-011: Simple camera projection shall be implemented:

- perspective projection for 3D view;
- optional orthographic projection for top-down debug view.

FR-RENDER-012: Renderer shall handle window resize correctly.

FR-RENDER-013: Renderer shall expose simple visual style parameters in config:

- road color;
- route color;
- building color;
- background color;
- marker size;
- building height scale.

7.7 Camera and Input Requirements
---------------------------------

FR-CAM-001: The app shall support both mouse and keyboard camera controls. Window/input library: moderngl-window.

Mouse controls:

- Left-button drag: orbit (rotate yaw/pitch around target)
- Right-button drag: pan (translate camera target in the horizontal plane)
- Scroll wheel: zoom in/out

Keyboard controls:

- W/S: move camera forward/backward
- A/D: move camera left/right
- Q/E: rotate camera left/right or move down/up
- Arrow keys: rotate/pitch camera
- Space: pause/resume vehicle animation
- R: reset marker to start
- F: cycle follow-camera sub-mode (orbit → close-follow → high-aerial → back to orbit)
- T: toggle top-down camera
- [ / ]: decrease/increase simulation speed by 10 km/h
- Esc: exit

FR-CAM-002: Initial camera shall show the whole route and nearby buildings.

FR-CAM-003: Follow mode has two sub-modes, cycled by pressing F:

Sub-mode A — Close follow (cinematic):
- Camera 20–30 m behind the marker, 10–15 m above road level.
- Perspective projection; field of view shows the road ahead and nearby buildings.

Sub-mode B — High aerial (map-watching):
- Camera 80–150 m above the marker, looking slightly downward.
- Always reveals the surrounding neighbourhood; marker appears small.

Both sub-modes rotate with the marker heading.

FR-CAM-004: Top-down mode shall place the camera above the route center.

7.8 Simulation Requirements
---------------------------

FR-SIM-000: The simulation shall start running immediately when the app opens. The marker begins moving from the route origin on the first frame.

FR-SIM-001: The simulation shall use elapsed real time and configurable speed to compute traveled distance.

FR-SIM-002: Position interpolation shall use cumulative route distance.

FR-SIM-003: Marker heading shall be computed from the route tangent.

FR-SIM-004: Marker shall wrap around when route distance exceeds total route length.

FR-SIM-005: Simulation speed shall be configurable from a config file and from runtime hotkeys. Keys [ and ] decrease/increase speed by 10 km/h, clamped to [5, 200] km/h.

FR-SIM-006: Marker visual height shall be slightly above road surface, e.g. y = 0.2 m.

FR-SIM-007: Marker shall be clearly visible in both oblique and top-down views.

7.9 UI / Overlay Requirements
-----------------------------

FR-UI-001: The app shall display FPS.

FR-UI-002: The app shall display route progress:

- distance traveled;
- total route length;
- speed in km/h;
- paused/running state.

FR-UI-003: The app shall display current camera mode.

FR-UI-004: The app shall display data counts in a debug panel or console log:

- road vertices/triangles;
- building vertices/triangles;
- number of buildings;
- route length.

FR-UI-005: For MVP, FPS is displayed in the window title. Route progress (FR-UI-002), camera mode (FR-UI-003), and data counts (FR-UI-004) are printed to the console at startup and on state changes — not rendered on-screen in MVP.

Postponed (post-MVP): in-window text overlay using bitmap font or ImGui. This must be tracked as a known limitation in README and CHANGELOG.

================================================================================
8. NON-FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

8.1 Performance
---------------

NFR-PERF-001: Target FPS shall be 60 FPS at 1280 x 720 with default Shinagawa dataset.

NFR-PERF-002: Minimum acceptable FPS shall be 30 FPS.

NFR-PERF-003: Renderer shall avoid creating large Python objects in the per-frame loop.

NFR-PERF-004: Renderer shall avoid rebuilding road/building geometry during normal playback.

NFR-PERF-005: Startup loading after preprocessing shall complete in under 10 seconds on a normal laptop for the default dataset.

NFR-PERF-006: Preprocessing may take longer, but should normally finish under 2 minutes for the default area.

NFR-PERF-007: Route simulation update shall take less than 1 ms per frame.

NFR-PERF-008: Total GPU draw calls should ideally be fewer than 20 per frame for MVP.

8.2 Reliability
---------------

NFR-REL-001: If OSM download fails, the script shall show a clear error message and suggest using cached data.

NFR-REL-002: If a building polygon fails triangulation/extrusion, it shall be skipped and counted.

NFR-REL-003: If route generation fails, the script shall save diagnostic information about snapped waypoints and graph connectivity.

NFR-REL-004: The app shall not crash if optional tags like height, lanes, width, or building:levels are missing.

8.3 Maintainability
-------------------

NFR-MAINT-001: Data processing and rendering code shall be separated.

NFR-MAINT-002: The rendering engine shall consume generic mesh buffers and should not know about raw OSM details.

NFR-MAINT-003: Projection logic shall be centralized in one module.

NFR-MAINT-004: Configurable constants shall not be hardcoded across many files.

NFR-MAINT-005: The code shall be ready for future replacement of Python preprocessing with C++/Rust if needed.

8.4 Portability
---------------

NFR-PORT-001: Primary target OS shall be Linux.

NFR-PORT-002: Secondary targets shall be Windows and macOS if ModernGL/window dependencies are available.

NFR-PORT-003: Avoid OS-specific APIs in MVP.

NFR-PORT-004: Python package versions shall be pinned or constrained.

8.5 Visual Quality
------------------

NFR-VIS-001: Roads shall be visibly distinct from background.

NFR-VIS-002: Route highlight shall be clearly visible above roads.

NFR-VIS-003: Buildings shall not obscure the marker completely in default view.

NFR-VIS-004: Camera near/far planes shall avoid clipping the map in default view.

NFR-VIS-005: Simple directional lighting or flat color shading is sufficient for MVP.

================================================================================
9. CONFIGURATION
================================================================================

Create config/shinagawa_poc.yaml with at least these fields:

area:
  name: "Tokyo Shinagawa PoC"
  bbox:
    north: 35.6420
    south: 35.6150
    east: 139.7600
    west: 139.7150
  center:
    lat: 35.6285
    lon: 139.7388

projection:
  source_epsg: 4326
  target_epsg: 32654
  local_origin: "bbox_center"

osm:
  use_cache: true
  cache_dir: "data/raw"
  network_type: "drive"
  building_tags:
    building: true

preprocess:
  output_dir: "data/processed/shinagawa"
  default_building_height_m: 9.0
  floor_height_m: 3.0
  max_building_height_m: 250.0
  road_y_m: 0.02
  route_y_m: 0.08
  marker_y_m: 0.20
  simplify_roads: true
  simplify_tolerance_m: 0.25

route:
  output_file: "data/routes/shinagawa_loop.json"
  desired_min_length_m: 4000
  desired_max_length_m: 5500
  default_speed_kmh: 40
  waypoints:
    - {name: "Shinagawa Station", lat: 35.6285, lon: 139.7388}
    - {name: "Sengakuji", lat: 35.6370, lon: 139.7380}
    - {name: "Konan", lat: 35.6265, lon: 139.7475}
    - {name: "Osaki/Gotanda side", lat: 35.6200, lon: 139.7285}
    - {name: "Shinagawa Station return", lat: 35.6285, lon: 139.7388}

render:
  window_width: 1280
  window_height: 720
  target_fps: 60
  background_color: [0.08, 0.09, 0.10, 1.0]
  road_color: [0.22, 0.22, 0.22, 1.0]
  route_color: [1.0, 0.55, 0.05, 1.0]
  building_color: [0.50, 0.53, 0.56, 1.0]
  marker_color: [0.1, 0.6, 1.0, 1.0]
  marker_length_m: 8.0
  marker_width_m: 4.0
  marker_height_m: 2.0
  camera_mode: "orbit"
  initial_camera_height_m: 1200
  initial_pitch_deg: 45
  initial_yaw_deg: 0
  follow_close_back_m: 25
  follow_close_up_m: 12
  follow_aerial_up_m: 120

logging:
  level: "INFO"

